==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|every day}}.

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# As frequently as [[every]] [[day]]; [[daily]].
#: {{syn|en|per diem}}
# Very frequently.
#: {{ux|en|[[it's not every day|It's not '''every day''']] you see that kind of thing.}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|it's not every day}}
* {{l|en|you learn something new every day}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|every day is a school day}}
* {{l|en|every day of the week}}
* {{l|en|every day of the week and twice on Sunday}}

====Translations====
{{trans-see|daily}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{obsolete form of|en|everyday}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1656|author=[[w:William Gurnall|Will{{quote-gloss|iam}} Gurnall]]|chapter=The third kinde of spiritual pride, ''viz.'' pride of Priviledges|title=The Christian in Compleat Armour. Or, A Treatise of the Saints War against the Devil,{{nb...|wherein a Discovery Is Made of That Grand Enemy of God and His People, in His Policies, Power, Seat of His Empire, Wickednesse, and Chief Design He Hath against the Saints. A Magazin Open’d: from Whence the Christian Is Furnished with Spiritual Armes for the Battel, Help’t on with His Armour, and Taught the Use of His Weapon, Together with the Happy Issue of the Whole Warre.}}|edition=2nd|volume_plain=1st part|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} Ralph Smith,{{nb...|at the Bible in Cornhill, near the Royall Exchange.}}|section=III|page=303|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_the-christian-in-complea_gurnall-william_1656/page/303/mode/1up|oclc=9591346|passage=The Comforter (’tis true) abides for ever in the Saints boſome, but his joys, they come and are gone again quickly. They are as exceedings, with which he feaſts the believer, but the cloth is ſoon drawn, and why ſo, but becauſe we cannot bear them for our '''every day''' food?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year='''1790''' (date written)|author=[[w:Gotthold Ephraim Lessing|G{{quote-gloss|otthold}} E{{quote-gloss|phraim}} Lessing]]|tlr={{quote-gloss|[[w:William Taylor (man of letters)|William Taylor]]}}|title=[[w:Nathan the Wise|Nathan the Wise, a Dramatic Poem,]]{{nb...|Written Originally in German by G. E. Lessing.}}|location=Norwich, Norfolk|publisher={{...|Printed by}} Stevenson and Matchett, {{...|in 1791; and published in 1805 by}} R. Philips,{{nb...|6, Bridge-Street, Blackfriars, London.}}|year_published=1805|page=20|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/nathanwiseadram00taylgoog/page/n32/mode/1up|oclc=11992460|passage=Well, and altho’ it sounds quite natural, an '''every day''' event, a simple story, that you was by a real templar sav’d, is it the less a miracle?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1797|author=w:Thomas Gisborne|chapter=On the Duties of Matrimonial Life|title=An Enquiry into the Duties of the Female Sex|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} [[w:Cadell & Davies|T{{quote-gloss|homas}} Cadell jun. and W{{quote-gloss|illiam}} Davies]]{{nb...|(Successors to Mr. [Thomas] Cadell) in the Strand.}}|page=258|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_an-enquiry-into-the-duti_gisborne-thomas_1797_0/page/258/mode/1up|oclc=5687101|passage=It {{quote-gloss|discretion}} is to the mind what the '''every day''' clothing is to the body, requiſite under every viciſſitude to health, and propriety, and comfort.}}

